{{define "content"}}
{{/* The security page is the product's central argument, not a legal
     document — it gets the marketing surface (ambient canvas, panels, the
     key-derivation diagram) rather than the legal sidebar. Every claim
     here must stay literally true of web/lib/crypto.ts and the Go side;
     see docs/security.md before editing the copy.

     This page answers the question a visitor arrives with: what happens to
     my data, and what does that stop. The construction behind the answer —
     the vocabulary, the parameters, the envelope, the threat model — lives
     at /whitepaper, and the two pages must never carry the same sentence.
     If a section here starts growing a second paragraph of mechanism, that
     paragraph belongs over there. */}}
{{/* Marked up as a technical article: this is the page we most want search
     and answer engines to quote, and quoting it correctly requires them to
     know it is documentation rather than a landing page. Keep dateModified
     in step with the "Last updated" line below. */}}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  "@id": "{{.CanonicalURL}}/#article",
  "headline": "Security",
  "description": "{{.PageDescription}}",
  "url": "{{.CanonicalURL}}",
  "dateModified": "2026-08-11",
  "inLanguage": "en-GB",
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "{{.Site.URL}}/#website" },
  "about": { "@id": "{{.Site.URL}}/#app" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "{{.Site.URL}}/#organization" },
  "proficiencyLevel": "Beginner",
  "citation": { "@id": "{{.Site.URL}}/whitepaper/#article" }
}
</script>
<div class="min-h-screen flex flex-col text-foreground overflow-x-hidden">
    <div class="site-canvas" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="glow-orb glow-orb-a" style="--orb:var(--accent-glow);width:58vw;height:58vw;top:-22vw;left:-12vw"></div>
        <div class="glow-orb glow-orb-b" style="--orb:var(--accent-2-glow);width:48vw;height:48vw;bottom:-22vw;right:-14vw"></div>
    </div>

    {{template "publicHeader" .}}

    {{/* ───────── Hero ───────── */}}
    <section class="relative px-6 pt-12 pb-10 sm:pt-20 sm:pb-16">
        <div class="glow-orb" style="--orb:var(--accent-3-glow);width:460px;height:320px;top:-90px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);opacity:0.7"></div>
        <div class="relative max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
            {{/* The page is called what it is. The badge used to say
                 "Security" above a headline that said the same thing in
                 more words; the headline says it now, and the badge is
                 gone rather than repeating it. */}}
            <h1 class="text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tighter leading-tight mb-6">
                <span class="text-gradient">Security</span>
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
                Everything in your vault is encrypted <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">on your device</strong>, with a key built from two secrets only you have. What the server stores is encrypted text, an email address and timestamps. Nothing that would open a vault is ever sent to it.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-2.5 font-mono text-[11px]">
                <span class="badge badge-accent-2">AES-256-GCM</span>
                <span class="badge badge-accent">Argon2id · 64 MiB</span>
                <span class="badge badge-accent-2">PBKDF2-SHA512 · 1,000,000</span>
                <span class="badge badge-accent">HKDF-SHA512</span>
            </div>
            {{/* The badges are the page's only jargon, and they are skippable
                 by design: the prose above and below never leans on them.
                 Anyone who wants them translated is one link away rather
                 than reading a glossary before the argument starts.

                 "Post-quantum" is deliberately NOT a fifth badge here — it is
                 a claim about the structure of the list, so it is stated as
                 one. See the copy rules in docs/product.md. */}}
            <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground max-w-xl mx-auto">
                Every primitive above is symmetric or hash-based. There is no public-key cryptography anywhere in Vault3, so there is nothing here for a quantum computer to break and nothing due to be replaced later. Each name is defined in <a href="/whitepaper" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-active">the whitepaper</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── Key derivation diagram ───────── */}}
    <section id="derivation" class="px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-reveal scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
            <div class="max-w-xl mb-8 sm:mb-12">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Key derivation</p>
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Two secrets, one key</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Unlocking your vault takes two things. Your browser combines them into a single unlock key and derives every other key from it, all before anything is sent.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="panel p-6 sm:p-10 scroll-reveal-child">
                {{/* Two secrets in ── */}}
                <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
                    <div class="flow-node">
                        <p class="font-semibold mb-1.5">Your Master Password</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Chosen by you, kept in your head, and never sent anywhere. Before it becomes part of a key it passes through two deliberate slowdowns, one after the other: a long run of standard arithmetic, then a stage that has to fill a block of memory for every attempt. Together they make large-scale guessing prohibitively expensive.</p>
                        <span class="badge badge-accent font-mono text-[11px]">Argon2id · 64 MiB · 4 passes ← PBKDF2-SHA512 · 1,000,000 · per-account salt</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-node">
                        <p class="font-semibold mb-1.5">Your Secret Phrase</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Twelve words your browser picks at random when you sign up, written into your Emergency Kit and kept on your device. No slowdown is applied here: the phrase is already uniformly random and long enough that searching it is infeasible, with enough margin to stay that way against a quantum computer.</p>
                        <span class="badge badge-accent font-mono text-[11px]">12 words · 132 bits · HKDF-SHA512</span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-merge hidden sm:block" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <div class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></div>

                {{/* ── combined into the master unlock key ── */}}
                <div class="flow-node flow-node-accent text-center">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-lg mb-1.5">Master unlock key</p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed max-w-lg mx-auto">Both secrets are required. A stolen phrase without the password, or a guessed password without the phrase, produces the wrong key.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <div class="flow-split hidden sm:block" aria-hidden="true"></div>

                {{/* ── split into two independent keys ── */}}
                <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
                    <div class="flow-node flow-node-cool">
                        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1.5">
                            <svg class="h-4 w-4 text-accent-2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="m14 6 6 6-6 6"/></svg>
                            <p class="font-semibold">Authentication key</p>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">This one is sent to the server, and its only job is to prove your identity. The server stores a hash of it rather than the key itself. It is a sibling of the encryption key rather than a copy, so it cannot decrypt anything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flow-node flow-node-accent">
                        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1.5">
                            <svg class="h-4 w-4 text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="11" width="18" height="11" rx="2"/><path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4"/></svg>
                            <p class="font-semibold">Encryption key</p>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">This one never leaves your device. It unwraps your vault key, which unwraps a separate key for each item, which decrypts that item's contents. Every item is sealed with <code class="font-mono text-accent">AES-256-GCM</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── What the server sees ───────── */}}
    <section class="px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
            <div class="max-w-xl mb-8 sm:mb-12">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Storage</p>
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">What the server stores</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Your item contents, item titles, vault names and wrapped keys all arrive already encrypted. Here is one item as it looks on your device, and as it is stored.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 scroll-reveal-child">
                <div class="panel p-6">
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-4">
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold">On your device</p>
                        <span class="badge badge-success font-mono text-[11px]">decrypted</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="compare-pane">
                        <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">title&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>Acme Bank</p>
                        <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">user&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>nora@fastmail.com</p>
                        <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">secret&nbsp;&nbsp;</span>tr7#Vk-pearl-9Q</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="panel p-6" data-security-cipher>
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-4">
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold">In the database</p>
                        <span class="badge badge-accent font-mono text-[11px]">sealed</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="compare-pane compare-pane-sealed">
                        <p data-cipher-line>hQ2nZpVt7aXeR4mKdJ0sLbYc9wUf…</p>
                        <p data-cipher-line>C8xNiO3vTgHq1jEzPr6kAyWm5uDb…</p>
                        <p data-cipher-line>Rf9LsQ4dBnM7tKcVaXo2eZuIhGp1…</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-8 max-w-2xl scroll-reveal-child">
                A copy of the whole database shows how many items you keep, when they last changed, and your email address. It does not show what any of them contain.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── What this makes impossible ───────── */}}
    <section class="px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
            <div class="max-w-xl mb-8 sm:mb-12">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">The consequences</p>
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Three things we cannot do</h2>
            </div>
            {{/* Three impossibilities, each shown as the artefact it denies:
                 the row a support agent would open, the button we cannot
                 offer, the haul a dump actually yields. */}}
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 sm:gap-6">
                <div class="panel panel-interactive p-6 sm:p-7 scroll-reveal-child flex flex-col">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold leading-snug mb-3">We cannot read your vault</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">There is no internal tool, support path or administrative override that decrypts a vault. The envelope below is the whole of what anyone here can open.</p>
                    <div class="mt-auto pt-6">
                        <div class="fig fig-sealed" aria-hidden="true">
                            <p class="fig-label mb-2">what a support request would show</p>
                            <p class="break-all">{"v":2,"alg":"A256GCM","n":"Vf7Kd3wZs2…","c":"hQ2nZpVt7aXeR4mKdJ0sLbYc9wUf…"}</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="panel panel-interactive p-6 sm:p-7 scroll-reveal-child">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold leading-snug mb-4">We cannot reset your Master Password</h3>
                    <div class="mb-4" aria-hidden="true">
                        <span class="mock-btn">
                            <svg class="h-4 w-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="m22 7-10 6L2 7"/></svg>
                            Email me a reset link
                        </span>
                        <p class="fig-label mt-2.5">this button does not exist</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">There is no reset email and no internal recovery route, including one that would wipe the vault so you could start over. The server holds encrypted text and a hash of your authentication key, and neither can be turned back into a working key. Your Emergency Kit is the way back in, which is why saving it is a required step at sign-up.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="panel panel-interactive p-6 sm:p-7 scroll-reveal-child">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold leading-snug mb-4">A breach of our servers cannot expose your secrets</h3>
                    <ul class="loot mb-4" aria-hidden="true">
                        <li>
                            <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 text-accent-2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
                            encrypted items
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 text-accent-2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
                            one-way hashes
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 text-accent-2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
                            encrypted account fields
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span class="deny-mark" style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem">
                                <svg class="h-3 w-3" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M18 6 6 18"/><path d="m6 6 12 12"/></svg>
                            </span>
                            one readable secret
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">An attacker who gets in leaves with encrypted items, one-way hashes and encrypted account fields. No readable copy of a secret is written anywhere, so there is none to find.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── Sharing ─────────
         The obvious objection to everything above: if we cannot read any of
         it, how can anyone else? This section answers only that — the
         security property. What you can actually share and for how long is
         /features's subject, and the construction is section 4 of the
         whitepaper. Keep all three apart. */}}
    <section class="px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
            <div class="max-w-xl mb-8 sm:mb-12">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Sharing</p>
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Sharing without handing over the key</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Vault3 can pass something to another person without ever holding what opens it. The key travels in the part of a link that browsers keep on the device and never transmit, so what reaches the server is an encrypted bundle and an opaque token.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 sm:gap-6">
                <div class="panel p-6 sm:p-7 scroll-reveal-child">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">What reaches the server</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">An opaque token, stored only as a hash. That is enough to expire a share or revoke one on request, and not enough to decrypt anything.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="panel p-6 sm:p-7 scroll-reveal-child">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2">What never does</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">The key. Your browser generates it, seals the share with it and writes it into the link. The recipient's browser strips it from the address bar as soon as it has been read, so it does not persist in their history.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-8 max-w-2xl scroll-reveal-child">
                Anyone holding a complete link can read what it opens. That is what sharing means, and cryptography does not change it. The narrower claim is the one being made here: <strong class="text-foreground">the server is not among them</strong>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── Honest limits ───────── */}}
    <section class="px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto panel p-7 sm:p-12 scroll-reveal-child">
            <div class="glow-orb" style="--orb:var(--accent-3-glow);width:400px;height:240px;top:-80px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);opacity:0.6"></div>
            <div class="relative flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6">
                <span class="icon-tile icon-tile-warm flex-shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg class="h-5 w-5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.3 3.2 1.8 17.5A2 2 0 0 0 3.5 20.5h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.2a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0Z"/><path d="M12 9v4"/><path d="M12 17h.01"/></svg>
                </span>
                <div>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">Honest limits</h2>
                    <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">All of this protects your vault from the server and from anyone who attacks it. It does not protect a device that is already compromised: software running on your computer can read whatever you have unlocked there, and encryption does not change that. So keep your devices updated, keep your Emergency Kit on paper rather than on the machine, and use a Master Password you use nowhere else.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── The design in full ─────────
         The handover to the whitepaper. One band rather than a card in the
         CTA row, because it is a destination rather than an afterthought —
         everything this page left out is behind it. */}}
    <section class="px-6 pb-12 sm:pb-16 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto panel p-7 sm:p-9 scroll-reveal-child">
            <div class="md:flex md:items-center md:justify-between md:gap-10">
                <div class="max-w-xl">
                    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Go deeper</p>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">The design, in full</h2>
                    <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Every parameter, the exact key hierarchy, how sharing is built, why a quantum computer finds nothing here to break, how to rebuild the cryptography module yourself and check it byte for byte, and the threat model including the parts that cannot be defended.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="mt-6 md:mt-0 flex-shrink-0">
                    <a href="/whitepaper" class="btn btn-secondary">Read the whitepaper &rarr;</a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{/* ───────── Report + CTA ───────── */}}
    <section class="px-6 pb-16 sm:pb-20 scroll-reveal">
        <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 sm:gap-6">
            <div class="panel p-7 sm:p-9 scroll-reveal-child">
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Found something?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-5">Security reports go straight to the people who build Vault3. There is no form and no triage queue; we read them ourselves and reply quickly.</p>
                <a href="mailto:{{.Site.ContactEmail}}" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm font-mono">{{.Site.ContactEmail}}</a>
            </div>
            <div class="cta-slab p-7 sm:p-9 flex flex-col justify-center scroll-reveal-child">
                <div class="glow-orb" style="--orb:var(--accent-glow);width:420px;height:260px;top:-140px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%)"></div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tighter mb-2">Now you know how it works.</h2>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-6">One password to remember. Twelve words to keep safe. Everything else, encrypted.</p>
                    <a href="/join" class="btn btn-primary">Create your vault</a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {{template "siteFooter" .}}
</div>
{{end}}
